# ── Server-side voice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to .env.local — ONE file at this directory serves everything: station,
# the scripts, the Next.js app in web/, and every room and research run station
# spawns as a child process (they inherit station's environment).
#
# station's CLI does not read dotfiles on its own, so lib/load-env.ts pulls this
# in from station.config.ts and the scripts, and web/next.config.ts pulls it in
# for the app. A var exported in your shell still wins over this file.

# Every key in this file stays in the gateway process. Unlike the browser-hosted
# example there are no NEXT_PUBLIC_* values and no token-minting routes: nothing
# here is ever sent to a client, because the client never talks to a provider.

# Speech in and out (ElevenLabs Scribe realtime + TTS streaming).
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=sk_...

# The models. Default provider is OpenRouter.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...

# ── S2S rooms (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The "s2s room" flavour replaces the whole cascaded pipeline with a Gemini
# Live speech-to-speech session (signals/s2s-room.ts): the realtime model IS
# the front agent's voice, and delegation to the worker still runs over the
# mesh as a research signal. Only needed if you claim s2s rooms.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=AI...
# S2S_MODEL=models/gemini-live-2.5-flash-preview
# S2S_VOICE=Puck

# ── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Provider for both agents (default: openrouter).
# VOICE_PROVIDER=openrouter

# Defaults on OpenRouter:
#   front  = moonshotai/kimi-k2-0905 (263ms TTFT via Groq; strong judgment about
#            which lines in the room were actually addressed to it)
#   worker = minimax/minimax-m2.5    (heavy tool work over the shop database)
# FRONT_MODEL=moonshotai/kimi-k2-0905
#
# Front-model choice is a genuine trade-off between judgment and latency, and
# scripts/front-eval.mjs measures both. Against the 11-case rubric (silence =
# how often it correctly says nothing in a hostile room), measured together:
#
#   kimi-k2-0905   9/11  silence 2/5   313ms to first spoken token
#   kimi-k2.6      9/11  silence 4/5  1853ms
#   claude-haiku-4.5  9/11  silence 5/5  3213ms
#
# The latency spread is mostly NOT the model: k2-0905 is served by Groq, and
# neither of the others has a Groq or Cerebras endpoint at all. Judgment costs
# roughly a second and a half of dead air before the first word, so the default
# stays on the fast one. Override per room with the `frontModel` input to
# compare without restarting anything.
# WORKER_MODEL=minimax/minimax-m2.5

# How hard the front model may think before speaking — silent reasoning is dead
# air on a voice line. low (default) | budget:<n> | off | none
# FRONT_REASONING=low

# OpenRouter provider routing for the front model. WHO serves the model matters
# more for latency than which model it is: measured on gpt-oss-120b, 979ms TTFT
# on default routing vs 160ms via sort=throughput on identical weights.
# Default: throughput. Options: throughput | latency | price | off.
# FRONT_PROVIDER_SORT=off

# ── Station auth (REQUIRED) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Station's API can start processes on this machine, so it is gated — station
# refuses to boot without these, and there is deliberately no default.
#
# These are for STATION itself: the dashboard login, and minting API keys.
# The web app never sees them.
STATION_USERNAME=admin
STATION_PASSWORD=change-me

# ── The web app's only credential ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Start station, then:
#   pnpm key "web app" trigger read cancel
# and put the result here (or in web/.env.local). Those three scopes are the
# whole room lifecycle — trigger a room, poll it, cancel it — all on /api/v1.
# STATION_API_KEY=sk_live_...

# Where station keeps its queue and dashboard.
# STATION_DB=./station.db

# The room pool. A room listens on ROOM_BASE_PORT + n, so the defaults below
# give you rooms on :4501-:4504. ROOM_SLOTS is both the port range and the
# `room` signal's concurrency cap — raise it for more simultaneous callers.
# ROOM_SLOTS=4
# ROOM_BASE_PORT=4500

# Hard ceiling on one call (default 1 hour). The runner SIGTERMs the room at
# this point and it closes gracefully — the backstop for a caller who never
# hangs up. Rooms also end themselves after 10 minutes with nobody attached.
# ROOM_MAX_MS=3600000

# Shared secret the worker presents when POSTing its mesh reply back to a room.
# MESH_TOKEN=dev-token

# ── Endpointing: how long after you stop talking before your turn is sent ────
# The right values depend on the room and how you pace. Every dispatch is
# logged to voice-metrics.jsonl with the reason the detector gave, so tune
# against `endpoint_hold` and `stt_dispatch_ms` rather than by feel.
#
# Which VAD decides when you start and stop talking. DEFAULT: the neural
# Silero model, run server-side through onnxruntime-node — it knows what speech
# SOUNDS like, so it catches a quiet "mm-hmm" and ignores a slammed door. The
# weights ship inside @ricky0123/vad-web (already a dependency): no download.
# Set "energy" to force the zero-dependency RMS fallback, which is also used
# automatically if the model fails to load.
# VOICE_VAD=energy
#
# Trailing silence before the VAD calls end-of-speech. The floor of send
# latency — nothing starts until this has passed.
# VAD_SILENCE_MS=450
# ENERGY VAD ONLY: RMS above which a frame counts as speech. Silero ignores
# this — it scores speech, not loudness.
# VAD_THRESHOLD=0.006
# The end-of-utterance model's confidence maps onto a hold between these two:
# certain-you're-done waits ~min, certain-you're-not waits ~max. Lower the max
# if it feels sluggish; raise the min if it cuts you off mid-thought.
# TURN_MIN_HOLD_MS=400
# TURN_MAX_HOLD_MS=2800

# The Next.js app (examples/server-voice/web) reads these:
#   STATION_API_KEY        its only station credential (see above)
#   STATION_URL            where to reach station's API (default http://localhost:4400)
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ROOM_HOST  what host the BROWSER uses to reach a room (default localhost)
#                          — set this when running behind a proxy or on another machine
#
# Everything else — the ElevenLabs voice, the TTS model, endpointing bounds, the
# idle window — is room INPUT, not env. It is declared in signals/room.ts and
# passed per room when the app triggers one, so it can differ per caller
# without touching the client, which has no opinion about any of it.
